      "sourceExcerpt": "Participants were allocated alternately by day. Outcome assessors knew allocation. At 8 weeks, 24/40 versus 20/40 participants improved. No prespecified protocol was provided.",
      "design": "non-randomized-interventional",
      "designNote": "Allocation was by alternation on days of entry. Alternate-day allocation is a systematic, predictable assignment rule and is not randomization, so no randomized design and no randomized-trial property is claimed for S1.",
      "instrument": null,
      "instrumentNote": "No appraisal instrument was applied to this excerpt; the ratings below are the platform's own structured judgement and are not a Rob 2, ROBINS-I or GRADE certification.",
      "domains": {
        "riskOfBias": {
          "rating": "serious",
          "reason": "Two steps named in the excerpt itself. Allocation: participants were assigned by alternation ('allocated alternately by day'), a predictable rule with no concealed sequence, so selection bias cannot be excluded. Detection: outcome assessors were not blinded ('Outcome assessors knew allocation'), so the 24/40 versus 20/40 improvement count could be influenced by knowledge of allocation. Reporting: no prespecified protocol was available, so selective outcome reporting could not be checked."
        },
        "indirectness": {
          "rating": "serious",
          "reason": "The question is about an adult population at 8 weeks and the excerpt gives no population at all, so population applicability cannot be established. The outcome is recorded only as participants who 'improved'; no definition, instrument, or minimal important difference is given, so the distance between the recorded outcome and a clinically meaningful improvement is undefined. The 8-week time point does match the question."
        },
        "imprecision": {
          "rating": "unclear",
          "reason": "The excerpt reports counts only, '24/40 versus 20/40 participants improved', with no confidence interval, variance or test, so the precision of the between-group difference cannot be judged from what was supplied."
        }
      }
    },
